d","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2010/020646s017lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"CNS: dizziness 27%, CNS: asthenia 23%, CNS: somnolence 21%, GI: nausea 11%, neurologic: tremor 9%, psychiatric: nervousness 8%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"CNS: dizziness 27%, CNS: asthenia 20%, CNS: somnolence 18%, GI: nausea 11%, neurologic: tremor 9%, psychiatric: nervousness 10%","rationale":"The FDA label pooled placebo-controlled add-on trial adverse-event table supports dizziness 27%, nausea 11%, tremor 9%, but lists asthenia 20%, somnolence 18%, and nervousness 10%, not 23%, 21%, and 8%.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2010/020646s017lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Precise antiseizure mechanism is unknown, but tiagabine blocks GABA uptake by the GAT-1 GABA transporter, increasing synaptic GABA availability.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"Precise antiseizure mechanism is unknown; tiagabine enhances GABA activity by binding to GABA uptake-carrier recognition sites and blocking GABA uptake into presynaptic neurons, increasing GABA available for postsynaptic receptor binding.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports GABA uptake-carrier blockade and increased GABA availability, but the cited FDA label wording does not specifically name GAT-1.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2010/020646s017lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"High","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"High","rationale":"GABA uptake inhibition is well established in FDA labeling and NCBI/PubMed pharmacology sources, despite the label noting that the precise antiseizure mechanism is unknown.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2010/020646s017lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9825084/"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA labeling","rationale":"FDA labeling supports the mechanism. DailyMed should not be needed as the named source when an FDA label URL is available.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2010/020646s017lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Not a major inducer/inhibitor; CYP3A substrate","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Not a major inducer/inhibitor; CYP3A substrate","rationale":"NCBI Bookshelf lists tiagabine as neither a hepatic enzyme inducer nor inhibitor, and a substrate affected by enzyme-inducing ASMs/CYP3A-mediated metabolism.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2597/?report=reader","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2513/table/A946/?report=objectonly","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9825084/"]},{"current_value":"Liver","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver","rationale":"LiverTox and NCBI pharmacokinetic sources state that tiagabine is metabolized by the liver/hepatic CYP system.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548376/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2597/?report=reader"]},{"current_value":"5-8 h; 2-3 h with enzyme inducers","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"5-8 h; 2-3 h with enzyme inducers","rationale":"PubMed review supports 5-8 h mean half-life and NCBI Bookshelf supports shortening to 2-3 h with hepatic enzyme-inducing ASMs.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9825084/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2597/?report=reader"]},{"current_value":"56 mg/day","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"56 mg/day","rationale":"FDA labeling state